WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.368
How SWE-bench-style grading works.

2
00:00:02.648 --> 00:00:11.480
SWE-bench (Jimenez and colleagues, 2023) built tasks from real GitHub issues in popular Python repositories.

3
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.987
Each task gives the agent the repository at the commit before the fix, plus the issue text.

4
00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:18.952
The agent produces a patch.

5
00:00:19.232 --> 00:00:29.301
Grading runs tests: tests that the real fix made pass must now pass (the issue is resolved), and tests that passed before must still pass (nothing else broke).

6
00:00:29.581 --> 00:00:33.443
“Resolved” therefore means “the hidden tests from the human fix pass.

7
00:00:33.723 --> 00:00:41.147
” It doesn’t mean the patch is the one a maintainer would accept, that it’s readable, or that it handles cases those tests don’t cover.

8
00:00:41.427 --> 00:00:59.005
SWE-bench Verified, built with OpenAI in 2024, is a 500-task subset that human annotators screened for clear problem statements and fair tests, because some original tasks were underspecified or had tests that rejected valid fixes.

9
00:00:59.285 --> 00:01:06.432
For about two years it was the headline coding number in model launches, and scores climbed from well under half to the high end.

10
00:01:06.712 --> 00:01:08.483
That climb is part of the story.

11
00:01:08.763 --> 00:01:18.640
When a benchmark’s scores bunch near the top, the remaining differences are a handful of tasks, and any flaw in those tasks matters more than the model differences you’re trying to see.
